<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#seven-wonders-of-the-ancient-world">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seven Wonders of the Ancient World</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Seven Wonders of the Ancient World are a list of monuments drawn up by Greek writers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a>: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-pyramid-of-giza">Great Pyramid of Giza</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanging-gardens-of-babylon">Hanging Gardens of Babylon</a>, the statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-artemis">Temple of Artemis at Ephesus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mausoleum-at-halicarnassus">Mausoleum at Halicarnassus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colossus-of-rhodes">Colossus of Rhodes</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lighthouse-of-alexandria">Lighthouse of Alexandria</a>. Only the Great Pyramid survives.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seven-wonders-of-the-ancient-world.jpg" alt="Seven Wonders of the Ancient World" width="320" height="528" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Mark22 at English Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SevenWondersOfTheWorld.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the conquests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> in the 4th century BCE, Hellenistic travellers gained access to the civilisations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a>, and began to list the landmarks that impressed them. The ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> spoke not of wonders but of theamata, meaning sights or things to be seen, so the list was the ancient counterpart of a travel guidebook. Later the word for wonder, thaumata, was used instead.</p><p>The first known list of seven dates to the 2nd to 1st century BCE, and at least eight full lists and ten partial lists are known. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> mentioned only the Walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and the Pyramids. The epigrammist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, who lived around or before 100 BCE, listed six of the present wonders and put the walls of Babylon where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lighthouse-of-alexandria">Lighthouse of Alexandria</a> now stands. Lists by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> and by the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callimachus">Callimachus</a> of Cyrene survive only as references.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The seven names now generally given are the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-pyramid-of-giza">Great Pyramid of Giza</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanging-gardens-of-babylon">Hanging Gardens of Babylon</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statue-of-zeus-at-olympia">Statue of Zeus at Olympia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-artemis">Temple of Artemis at Ephesus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mausoleum-at-halicarnassus">Mausoleum at Halicarnassus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colossus-of-rhodes">Colossus of Rhodes</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lighthouse-of-alexandria">Lighthouse of Alexandria</a>. This canonical list was first established in the 1572 publication Octo Mundi Miracula from a combination of historical sources. Two wonders stood in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, two in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, two in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and one in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. Five of the seven celebrate Greek construction; the exceptions are the pyramids and the Hanging Gardens.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Only the Great Pyramid survives, and it lost its white stone facing around 1300 when local communities removed it for building. The Colossus, finished after 280 BCE, fell to an earthquake in 226 or 225 BCE, so all seven stood together for less than sixty years. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-artemis">Temple of Artemis</a> and the statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> were destroyed by fire, and the Lighthouse and the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mausolus">Mausolus</a> by earthquakes.</p><p>Sculptures from the tomb and the temple are in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>. Scholars debate the nature of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanging-gardens-of-babylon">Hanging Gardens</a> and whether they existed. Later lists added Roman and Christian sites, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a> named the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noah">Noah</a>'s Ark. Wilhelm Heinrich Roscher found 22 distinct places across 18 classical lists in 1906.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Seven Wonders of the Ancient World” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The archaic temple, begun in the mid-sixth century BCE by the architects Chersiphron and Metagenes with columns paid for by Croesus, was among the first great marble temples of the Ionic order, and its fourth-century successor was counted among the <b>Seven Wonders of the Ancient World</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It was home to the Library of Alexandria and the Mouseion, which attracted scholars such as Euclid, Eratosthenes and Herophilus, and to the Pharos lighthouse, one of the <b>Seven Wonders</b> of the ancient world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire">Ptolemaic Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…a bronze statue of Helios by Chares of Lindos, said to have been paid for by selling the siege engines left by Demetrius Poliorcetes, stood at the harbour from about 280 BCE until the earthquake brought it down; it was counted among the <b>Seven Wonders of the World</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Great Pyramid is the oldest of the <b>Seven Wonders of the Ancient World</b> and the only one that has remained largely intact; Cyriacus of Ancona could already testify in the 15th century that the pyramids of Giza alone among the wonders had survived.</blockquote><a href="/work/great-pyramid-of-giza">Great Pyramid of Giza · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>After his death his sister-wife Artemisia II completed his tomb, the Mausoleum, one of the <b>Seven Wonders</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/hecatomnid-caria">Hecatomnid Caria</a></li></ul></section>
